**Set-off (any exporter/importer)**: an authorised dealer may set off **outstanding export receivables** against **outstanding import payables** with the **same overseas party**, or among **group and associate companies** through a **centralised settlement arrangement**, net or gross, if:

- **one dealer** operates and supervises it;
- the dealer is satisfied on **bona fides, KYC/AML**; invoices are **not under investigation**;
- **ACU** transactions stay **outside**;
- **goods are not set off against services**;
- **both legs are outstanding** and fall in the **same calendar year**;
- a **verifiable agreement** (bilateral) or a **written enforceable contract** (group) supports it; no **tax avoidance**;
- each export and import is **still reported gross** in the monitoring systems, using the **set-off indicator**.

**Netting for SEZ units (narrower)**: only **bilateral** with the **same overseas buyer/supplier**, **as at the balance sheet date**; exports on **EDF/DTR**, imports on **A1/A2**; the export form is **treated as complete only after the entire proceeds are adjusted or received**.

When a foreign buyer **pays in advance**, the exporter must:

- **ship within three years** of receiving it;
- pay interest, if any, of **not more than the benchmark rate + 100 basis points**;
- **route the shipping documents through the dealer that received the advance**.

After **three years**, **no refund or interest can be remitted without RBI approval**.

**Long-term advance (up to 10 years)**, for long supply contracts, needs an exporter with **at least three years' satisfactory record** and conditions such as: **firm irrevocable orders**, a **vetted contract**, **capacity to execute**, a **clean enforcement record**, **adjustment through future exports**, interest **at most benchmark + 200 bp**, **documents through one dealer**, **no use to clear NPA rupee loans**, **no double financing**, and **immediate reporting to RBI of an advance of USD 100 million or more**.
